WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Welcome. This class is about making AI
output inspectable before

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.000
anyone depends on it. You will choose
examples that reveal

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.000
a rule instead of hiding it. You will
define an

4
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.000
output contract for either a person or an
application. You

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.000
will distinguish requested formatting,
valid JSON, and schema-constrained output.

6
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Then you will validate facts, business
rules, and edge cases

7
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.000
after the structure passes. The result is
not a promise

8
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:40.000
that a model will always be correct. It is

9
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.000
a workflow that makes different kinds of
failure visible.

10
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.333
Examples can demonstrate tone,
classification boundaries, field

11
00:00:50.333 --> 00:00:55.667
population, and handling of unusual
inputs. They are most useful

12
00:00:55.667 --> 00:01:01.000
when the governing rule is also stated. If
you show

13
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.333
only one easy example, a model may imitate
accidental features:

14
00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:11.667
the length, wording, order, or label
balance. A reviewer may

15
00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:17.000
also be unable to tell which feature was
intentional. Start

16
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:22.333
with the rule, then choose a small set
that challenges

17
00:01:22.333 --> 00:01:27.667
it. For a support-ticket classifier,
include a normal billing case,

18
00:01:27.667 --> 00:01:33.000
a boundary between billing and account
access, and a missing-information

19
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:38.333
case that must be escalated. Explain why
each expected label

20
00:01:38.333 --> 00:01:43.667
follows from the rule. Avoid examples
containing secrets or personal

21
00:01:43.667 --> 00:01:49.000
data merely because they are realistic.
Replace sensitive details with

22
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:54.333
safe, representative fixtures. Finally,
keep examples independent from evaluation

23
00:01:54.333 --> 00:01:59.667
cases. If every test repeats the examples,
you only learn

24
00:01:59.667 --> 00:02:05.000
that the system can reproduce what it has
already seen.

25
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:09.333
Suppose the rule is: label a request
account-access only when

26
00:02:09.333 --> 00:02:13.667
the user cannot authenticate or cannot
reach an authorized account;

27
00:02:13.667 --> 00:02:18.000
label a request billing when access works
but the charge,

28
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.333
invoice, or payment is disputed; escalate
when both are present

29
00:02:22.333 --> 00:02:26.667
or essential facts are missing. A weak
example set contains

30
00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:31.000
three obvious password-reset requests. A
stronger set includes: I cannot

31
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.333
sign in after changing phones, expected
account-access; I can sign

32
00:02:35.333 --> 00:02:39.667
in but the invoice shows the wrong
quantity, expected billing;

33
00:02:39.667 --> 00:02:44.000
and I cannot sign in and need to dispute a

34
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.333
charge, expected escalation. The third
example demonstrates the conflict rule

35
00:02:48.333 --> 00:02:52.667
rather than forcing one convenient label.
I also add a

36
00:02:52.667 --> 00:02:57.000
case with no description and expect
escalation for missing evidence.

37
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.333
Now a reviewer can inspect whether the
examples cover the

38
00:03:01.333 --> 00:03:05.667
rule instead of merely sharing vocabulary.
The examples guide behavior,

39
00:03:05.667 --> 00:03:10.000
but the written rule remains the
authority.

40
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:15.333
An output contract defines what a usable
result contains before

41
00:03:15.333 --> 00:03:20.667
generation begins. Name the purpose and
who consumes it. Choose

42
00:03:20.667 --> 00:03:26.000
prose, a table, or a named schema. List
required fields

43
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:31.333
or sections, allowed values, evidence
requirements, ordering, length limits, and

44
00:03:31.333 --> 00:03:36.667
behavior when a value is unknown. For a
human reader,

45
00:03:36.667 --> 00:03:42.000
headings and a checklist may be
sufficient. For an application,

46
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:47.333
define stable field names, types,
enumerated values, null behavior, and

47
00:03:47.333 --> 00:03:52.667
versioning. Keep portable meaning separate
from provider configuration. The business

48
00:03:52.667 --> 00:03:58.000
contract might require category,
confidence band, evidence excerpt, and

49
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:03.333
escalation reason. One provider may
enforce that contract through

50
00:04:03.333 --> 00:04:08.667
structured-output settings; another may
support a different schema subset. The

51
00:04:08.667 --> 00:04:14.000
application still owns validation and
authorization. Unknown handling deserves

52
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:19.333
special care. Null, omitted, unknown, and
not applicable have different

53
00:04:19.333 --> 00:04:24.667
meanings. Pick one deliberately instead of
letting an absent value

54
00:04:24.667 --> 00:04:30.000
become a convincing default.

55
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.000
Choose a recurring task such as issue
triage, meeting-summary extraction,

56
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:40.000
or product comparison. Write the result's
user and purpose. Then

57
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:45.000
list three required fields, one
allowed-value rule, one evidence

58
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:50.000
requirement, and the exact representation
of an unknown value. Pause

59
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:55.000
before deciding which model or API feature
will produce it.

60
00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:30.313
Formatting, parseable data, and schema
adherence are different guarantees. A

61
00:06:30.313 --> 00:06:35.625
prompt may request JSON, yet the response
can include commentary,

62
00:06:35.625 --> 00:06:40.938
omit fields, or change types. A mode that
guarantees syntactically

63
00:06:40.938 --> 00:06:46.250
valid JSON establishes that a JSON parser
can read the

64
00:06:46.250 --> 00:06:51.563
text. It does not necessarily prove that
required fields exist

65
00:06:51.563 --> 00:06:56.875
or match your business schema. A supported
schema-constrained feature is

66
00:06:56.875 --> 00:07:02.188
designed to enforce the declared structure
and allowed types, but

67
00:07:02.188 --> 00:07:07.500
providers and models differ in supported
schema keywords, refusal behavior,

68
00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:12.813
limits, and interfaces. Test the exact
deployment you will use.

69
00:07:12.813 --> 00:07:18.125
Even perfect schema adherence does not
prove factual correctness, source

70
00:07:18.125 --> 00:07:23.438
support, completeness for the real
decision, or permission to act.

71
00:07:23.438 --> 00:07:28.750
Treat the gates as layers: transport
received a response; parsing

72
00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:34.063
produced data; schema validation
established shape; business validation

73
00:07:34.063 --> 00:07:39.375
checked meaning; evidence validation
checked support; authorization decided

74
00:07:39.375 --> 00:07:44.688
whether any consequence may follow. Log
which layer failed without

75
00:07:44.688 --> 00:07:50.000
exposing sensitive content.

76
00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:55.000
Checkpoint. A response parses as JSON and
matches the declared

77
00:07:55.000 --> 00:08:00.000
schema, but its evidence excerpt comes
from the wrong source

78
00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:05.000
and its recommended action exceeds the
user's authority. Which validation

79
00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:10.000
layers passed, which failed, and may the
application perform the

80
00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:15.000
action?

81
00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:33.500
The JSON parser and schema validator
passed. The evidence layer

82
00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:37.000
failed because the excerpt does not
support the claim, and

83
00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:40.500
the authorization layer failed because the
user cannot approve the

84
00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:44.000
action. The application must not perform
it. It should preserve

85
00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:47.500
a safe error record, return a reviewable
failure, and route

86
00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:51.000
the case according to policy. If you
answered that schema

87
00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:54.500
success made the object valid, revise your
language: schema-valid describes

88
00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:58.000
shape only. If you proposed replacing the
evidence with a

89
00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:01.500
fabricated value, stop. Missing or invalid
evidence must remain visible

90
00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:05.000
rather than becoming authoritative-looking
data.

91
00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:10.000
Content validation begins after parsing.
Check required business relationships, not

92
00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:15.000
only individual fields. A start date may
be valid text

93
00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:20.000
but precede the request date. A confidence
value may be

94
00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:25.000
a valid number but outside the allowed
range. A cited

95
00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:30.000
source may exist but not support the
claim. A classification

96
00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:35.000
may be allowed but contradict the supplied
evidence. Test normal,

97
00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:40.000
boundary, missing, conflicting, refusal,
truncated, and malicious-input cases.

98
00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:45.000
Handle each failure explicitly. A refusal
is not a malformed

99
00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:50.000
success record. An incomplete response is
not permission to invent

100
00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:55.000
required fields. A parse error is not a
reason to

101
00:10:55.000 --> 00:11:00.000
retry forever. Bound retries, preserve the
original safe evidence, and

102
00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:05.000
route unresolved cases to a person or
recovery path. Validate

103
00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:10.000
downstream consequences too. Spreadsheet
formulas, markup, URLs, commands, and tool

104
00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:15.000
arguments need context-specific safety
checks even when their container is

105
00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:20.000
valid. The output contract tells you what
to inspect; it

106
00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:25.000
does not transfer responsibility to the
model.

107
00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:29.643
Consider an issue-triage object with
category, urgency, evidence, and next

108
00:11:29.643 --> 00:11:34.286
action. The sample parses and matches the
schema. First pass:

109
00:11:34.286 --> 00:11:38.929
all fields exist, category is one of
incident, request, question,

110
00:11:38.929 --> 00:11:43.571
or unknown, and urgency is an integer from
one to

111
00:11:43.571 --> 00:11:48.214
five. Structure passes. Second pass: the
evidence says the user

112
00:11:48.214 --> 00:11:52.857
is asking how a feature works, but
category is incident

113
00:11:52.857 --> 00:11:57.500
and urgency is five. The next action says
disable the

114
00:11:57.500 --> 00:12:02.143
production service, although the workflow
only permits drafting a support

115
00:12:02.143 --> 00:12:06.786
response. Content consistency and
authorization fail. The correct result is

116
00:12:06.786 --> 00:12:11.429
a rejected candidate with reasons, not an
automatic incident and

117
00:12:11.429 --> 00:12:16.071
not a repaired object invented by the
validator. I add

118
00:12:16.071 --> 00:12:20.714
this case to the held-out evaluation set.
The example teaches

119
00:12:20.714 --> 00:12:25.357
that validators should preserve evidence
and make failure inspectable rather

120
00:12:25.357 --> 00:12:30.000
than silently forcing data into an allowed
shape.

121
00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:35.625
Open the output-contract activity. Choose
issue triage, meeting extraction, product

122
00:12:35.625 --> 00:12:41.250
comparison, or another recurring task.
Define purpose, consumer, required fields,

123
00:12:41.250 --> 00:12:46.875
allowed values, evidence, unknown
handling, ordering, and validation. Create

124
00:12:46.875 --> 00:12:52.500
a normal example, a boundary example, and
a missing-information example

125
00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:58.125
tied to an explicit rule. Then review a
sample response

126
00:12:58.125 --> 00:13:03.750
twice: first for parsing and schema, then
for factual support,

127
00:13:03.750 --> 00:13:09.375
business consistency, consequences, and
authority. Save the contract, three

128
00:13:09.375 --> 00:13:15.000
examples, and the two-part checklist.

129
00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:19.167
When you are ready, begin the knowledge
check. You will

130
00:16:19.167 --> 00:16:23.333
choose varied examples, identify
output-contract elements, distinguish

131
00:16:23.333 --> 00:16:27.500
valid JSON from schema and content
validity, handle refusals or

132
00:16:27.500 --> 00:16:31.667
incomplete output, and diagnose evidence
failure inside a schema-valid response.

133
00:16:31.667 --> 00:16:35.833
Review the class or activity before
submitting. The assessment opens

134
00:16:35.833 --> 00:16:40.000
only when you choose Begin knowledge
check.

135
00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:45.000
Keep the layers separate: examples
illustrate a rule, contracts define

136
00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:50.000
a usable shape, parsers and schemas test
structure, and accountable

137
00:16:50.000 --> 00:16:55.000
validation tests truth, consequences, and
authority.
